  "hints": [
    {
      "tier": 1,
      "text": "Start with the calendar. You are told January 29 is a Thursday, and the calendar shows three consecutive day columns. Work out which English weekday each column heading must be, then look at what the five words in A1 have in common.",
      "xpCost": 1
    },
    {
      "tier": 2,
      "text": "Almost every word here is two words stuck together. Split each one and translate the pieces separately: kondre is a place, man is a person, buku is a book, dosu is a box. The order matters — the modifier comes first, so asiwagi is horse-wagon, not wagon-horse.",
      "xpCost": 2
    },
    {
      "tier": 3,
      "text": "Worked example: tudewroko. Break it into tu + de + wroko. From the calendar you know tu is two. So this is two-day-work. Sranan counts work days from the start of the week, so two-day-work is Tuesday and dridewroko is Wednesday. Apply the same splitting to fodewroko, then to the compounds in A2 and A3.",
      "xpCost": 3
    }
  ],
  "explanationHtml": "<p>Everything in this problem turns on one observation: Sranan Tongo builds new words by gluing old ones together, and the modifier always comes first.</p><h3>The calendar</h3><p>The calendar shows three consecutive columns headed <span class=\"data\">freida</span>, <span class=\"data\">satra</span>, <span class=\"data\">sondo</span> — Friday, Saturday, Sunday, all recognisably borrowed from English or Dutch. The numbers underneath give you the counting words: <span class=\"data\">tu</span> 2, <span class=\"data\">dri</span> 3, <span class=\"data\">fo</span> 4, <span class=\"data\">neigi</span> 9, <span class=\"data\">tin</span> 10, <span class=\"data\">erfu</span> 11.</p><h3>Weekdays</h3><p><span class=\"data\">munde</span> and <span class=\"data\">sonde</span> are direct borrowings: Monday and Sunday. The other three end in <span class=\"data\">-dewroko</span>, literally <em>day-work</em>. Sranan numbers the working days: <span class=\"data\">tudewroko</span> is <em>two-day-work</em>, and since the week's work starts on Monday, that lands on Tuesday. So <span class=\"data\">dridewroko</span> is Wednesday and <span class=\"data\">fodewroko</span> is Thursday.</p><h3>Compounds</h3><p>Once you can split words, the rest falls out. The recurring pieces are:</p><ul><li><span class=\"data\">kondre</span> country, <span class=\"data\">man</span> person, <span class=\"data\">pikin</span> small or child, <span class=\"data\">tongo</span> tongue or language</li><li><span class=\"data\">buku</span> book, <span class=\"data\">dosu</span> box, <span class=\"data\">frei</span> fly, <span class=\"data\">faya</span> fire, <span class=\"data\">gado</span> god</li><li><span class=\"data\">wagi</span> wagon, <span class=\"data\">kanti</span> edge, <span class=\"data\">gron</span> ground or field, <span class=\"data\">wroko</span> work</li></ul><p>So <span class=\"data\">pokudosu</span> is <em>music-box</em>, which is a radio; <span class=\"data\">wortubuku</span> is <em>word-book</em>, a dictionary; <span class=\"data\">gadokondre</span> is <em>god-country</em>, heaven; <span class=\"data\">pikintongo</span> is <em>small-tongue</em>, the uvula.</p><p>Two are worth pausing on. <span class=\"data\">bakra</span> means a white person, so <span class=\"data\">bakratongo</span> is <em>white-person-tongue</em> — Dutch, the colonial language — and <span class=\"data\">bakrakondre</span> is <em>white-person-country</em>, the Netherlands. And <span class=\"data\">manpikin</span> is <em>man-small</em>: a male child, a son. Note the order flips the meaning against <span class=\"data\">pikin kaw</span>, <em>small cow</em>, a calf.</p><p>The title is the giveaway. <span class=\"data\">asiwagi</span> is <em>horse-wagon</em>: the horse comes before the cart.</p>",
  "reviewStatus": "published"
}
